# Debate on the White Paper on Local Government
Tamil title: உள்ளாட்சி பற்றிய வெள்ளை அறிக்கையின் மீதான விவாதம்

Speech 8, delivered on 5 November 1957.

**C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Speaker, the Finance Minister has said that this debate on the White Paper is not merely a stage at which we accept or reject conclusions already reached, but an opportunity to examine whether the decisions we intend to take are needed by the country. I welcome that statement with considerable hope.

I say that I welcome it hopefully because, as the honourable Chinnadurai pointed out, an experiment resembling the conditions the White Paper seeks has already been conducted at a place in Madurai district. This can create the suspicion that a decision has already been taken and that a debate is being held here only for form's sake. [Part of the sentence is damaged in OCR.] I welcome the Government's assurance that these are not conclusions we must simply accept.

The proposed method is confusing. The confusion exists not merely among listeners but to a great extent among those who explain it.

The idea that local institutions should be divided and reorganised was urged in this country as early as the period of Rathinasabapathy Mudaliar, before anyone was considering community development programmes. The task is not merely to examine whether a new intermediate arrangement is suitable today. I fear this issue is being approached with the intention that either the existing or proposed local institutions should be used as instruments for carrying out community development programmes.

The Finance Minister gave me reason for that fear. He devoted twenty minutes of his address to emphasising the community development programme and argued that for it to function properly villagers must show concern, possess an interest in it, and have facilities for it within their organisations.

In our region, it is customary for someone who goes to buy cattle also to look for a bride. In the same way, it is as though the Government, while going to purchase cattle, has decided to bring back a woman too. While examining how to implement the community development programme successfully, it has attached this long-standing question of local government to it.

We can sincerely welcome the Government's concern that the community development programme should function well. But I genuinely fear that just as the Planning Commission has grown to a form capable of directing the Central Government and has consequently caused many permanent financial institutions to end their lives prematurely, the machinery of community development here may completely destroy the permanent machinery of local institutions.

Plans may be regarded as extremely important by today's rulers. Yet the permanent Government machinery among us is no less important. Newly created planning machinery should not be shaped so that it wholly destroys permanent Government institutions or brings the two into conflict. I wish to make that view clear.

At every stage, we have seated officials beside representatives elected by the public, as though the elected members must be distrusted. What I truly fear from placing officials in this manner is what Dr Soundaram explained yesterday: people of good quality will hesitate to serve in such bodies.

At a panchayat conference I attended, panchayat presidents and members spoke openly of the repulsive manner in which officials behaved when approached. They passed a resolution that officials should no longer conduct themselves that way. A panchayat president who spoke during that resolution described with great emotion the suffering they endured at the hands of officials. The Minister cannot understand it merely from my retelling. Because I heard it directly and understood it, I have come forward to present that suffering in this House.

The distress already caused by officials is more than enough. [Several lines are damaged.] Because the Minister's argument invited an explanation of the underlying principles, I am obliged to say a few words about them.

Installing officials everywhere in order to administer panchayats or local bodies will only enlarge the official class; it will not establish the people's right to self-government.

If a ten-rupee note is exchanged into coins and each person receives a share, everyone receives usable money. Our Government instead tears a ten-rupee note into ten pieces and gives one piece at each level. It gives one piece to a panchayat of five representatives for five hundred people. It takes another piece and entrusts it to a panchayat circle formed by joining ten villages so that they may perform certain tasks. It combines seventy or eighty panchayats and gives another piece to a region based on the area where development programmes now operate.

Authority is thus fragmented until it cannot be exercised anywhere. No institution receives the full opportunity to carry out its work. Local administrative authority is entrusted to Block Development Officers and *Gram Sevaks*. Those officials will focus upon implementing rural welfare and development schemes; they will not show concern for cultivating a spirit of self-government within panchayats.

The Government of India itself has not boasted about Block Development Officers or community development programmes. More than that, its own Evaluation Committee report has shown that the task undertaken is not being conducted properly. The honourable Mr Dey and many members of Parliament have pointed out that Block Development Officers have not properly discharged their duties.

If administration is conducted through officers who remain under suspicion and have not yet been found to perform their work properly, they will truly ruin democracy. I repeat, therefore, that representatives elected by the public must be given adequate opportunity everywhere.

The Minister says district boards cover areas so vast and unmanageable that they cannot perform their work, and that administration will now be conducted in smaller units. I wish to offer him a small suggestion. Instead of adopting another method to determine the size of those units, why should Legislative Assembly constituencies, already demarcated for political purposes, not be used as one basis?

In discussing such matters, two English expressions are commonly used. One says a unit must be an “economic unit” for efficient administration: it must possess the means to provide every economic facility. The other says it must be a “political unit,” an arrangement suited to political administration.

The proposed panchayat units are neither economic nor political units. [A portion of the explanation is damaged.] Existing constituencies could be used as the basis for division. Ignoring both principles and joining two existing blocks into some kind of unit resembles a blind man throwing his staff and a lame man placing his foot wherever it falls. It does not appear appropriate.

Village people do not know whom they should approach, for what purpose or by what means. Many different sets of officials have been appointed. The Revenue Department digs wells. A Local Development Department also digs wells. Wells are dug under the community development programme too.

In one place in my constituency, a well was dug without a parapet wall. When asked why the wall had not been built, Revenue officials reportedly said, “We did not dig it; the community development programme did. Ask them.” The others replied, “We did not come; you must ask those people.” Even today, villagers use that well without a protective wall.

Such disorder occurs because the public does not support the work. I am pleased to hear the Finance Minister say that community development programmes can succeed only when the people cooperate, support them and participate.

Had he examined the same principle a little further, he could have understood when the public gives support and when it refuses. Wherever the people believe that a community development project will be properly conducted and is a good scheme, they enthusiastically support and participate in it. Wherever they conclude that the scheme will not be conducted properly and is merely eyewash or a coat of polish, support is absent.

With your permission, Mr Speaker, I wish to narrate an incident I have seen in practice.

At Soundappattu village near Kavundapadi in the Finance Minister's Coimbatore district, more than forty-seven houses were built for tribal people under a community development scheme. I heard that the Finance Minister himself was to inaugurate them. But he did not go, because within a short time of construction they had become too dilapidated for him to open. Of the forty-seven houses, twenty-six had fallen and lay in ruins.

I was invited to see them. In my speech I remarked, “It is better that you invited me to witness this ruin instead of inviting the Finance Minister.” I ask the Minister to visit the place without fail when he goes to that district.

The public's cooperation can be obtained only if community development programmes are executed properly and inspire confidence that they are not eyewash. The method and policy needed to obtain that cooperation are different. I do not agree with attaching them to local institutions.

The Finance Minister said that linking the motion of one machine to another could break one or interfere with the operation of the other. An example can be seen in his own district. Pneumatic tyres are fitted to bullock carts. A cart with such tyres may glide on a tar road, but fitting them to a cart travelling on an earthen road means joining incompatible things. I ask the Government not to follow that method everywhere.

It is not right to bind together the reorganisation of local bodies and the success of community development programmes. This is the fundamental defect I see in the report presented from that perspective.

There is also distrust concerning the White Paper's other principles. Speakers from every party—except Mr Muthiah—have not welcomed them. Mr Muthiah said he welcomed them only to some extent. Although he did not explain that extent here, perhaps he will do so in the Select Committee. I trust that the Select Committee will carefully and deeply examine every matter worthy of thought.

The Finance Minister also offered all political parties a suggestion concerning panchayat elections. He asked them to consider whether it is good for party feeling to develop there and whether candidates should contest panchayat elections on behalf of political parties.

Through you, Mr Speaker, I wish to tell the Finance Minister one thing. The party to which I formerly belonged once laid down the rule that candidates should not contest local bodies under party banners. It was Congress that destroyed that method and introduced party-based contests in local institutions.

If Congress now wishes to establish a rule that political parties should not contest—and to create what English calls a “gentlemen's agreement”—and if all political parties assemble and reach that decision, I state today that the party to which I belong is prepared to cooperate fully with such a Congress proposal.

I say this trusting that it represents the Congress party's view. If it is merely the Finance Minister's personal opinion, I respect him for expressing an elevated view; the nature of the action he takes upon it will become known only in the future.

Contesting panchayat elections under political-party labels might at least produce a civilised contest. Panchayat elections will in any event occur through some kind of faction. Anyone who visits villages can see it: North Street is one party and South Street another, and they quarrel; Upper Street and East Street quarrel; factions fight in the name of caste; or the great household and small household oppose one another. [Several lines are damaged.] A contest between political parties may be preferable to these divisions.

Congress's present wish to prevent party contests may reflect a change of heart, or it may be because results in many places brought Congress greater losses than expected. Whatever the reason, if the idea arose because it is good, our party will cooperate. I welcome an arrangement for all parties to meet and discuss it.

I wish to emphasise that the reform we undertake must empower local institutions and enable them to function properly. It should not be based upon a temporary community-development consideration.

Community development programmes are framed for periods of five years; they are not permanent. The First Five-Year Plan has ended and the Second has been prepared. If possible, they may introduce a Third Plan and, if permitted, even a Fourth. These are schemes for specified years, not permanent arrangements. Local institutions, however, form a permanent system.

Binding a temporary programme to a permanent institution resembles placing a little tin inside another tin. It will not fit; it will only make noise from within and provide no security.

The question of local institutions is one matter and the question of community development programmes another. It is wrong to bind the two together. I conclude with the hope that the Legislative Assembly committee will consider how local bodies may be properly reorganised.

---NOTES---
The OCR date “05.17.1957” is invalid. The filename `ullatchi051157` and chronological context support 5 November 1957; the source remains verbatim.

“ஸ்தல ஸ்தாபனங்கள்” is the period expression for local bodies or local institutions. “சமுதாய நலத் திட்டம்” is rendered as the Community Development Programme in the administrative context of Block Development Officers and Gram Sevaks.

Several lines in images 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 and 9 are severely damaged. The translation marks or conservatively bridges only passages whose meaning is established by the surrounding argument.

The cattle-and-bride analogy is translated as spoken; it criticises the Government for attaching a separate, long-standing local-government question to its immediate development-programme review.
